## 6. Issues and recommendations

### 6.1 Dashboard design

1. **Stray text on the title box.** The title text box has a container title set to `ss`, which looks like a leftover placeholder. Clear it.
2. **Typo in the matrix title.** It reads `Avg Delivery Time by City and Hour”.` with a stray curly quote and full stop.
3. **Line chart has no visible title or axis labels.** Add "Monthly GMV (₹)" so the reader knows what it shows. The card visuals also have empty titles, so the KPIs depend on the card's own label.
4. **No currency or unit formatting is configured**, so GMV may show as a raw number. Format it as ₹ in millions and add "min" to delivery time.
5. **Single page only.** Consider adding a cancellations/refunds view and a customer-segment view, since the model already supports them.
6. **Matrix readability.** 24 hours × 5 cities is a good candidate for conditional formatting (a heat map) to make the peak-hour pattern obvious at a glance.

### 6.2 Data model

1. **Five auto date tables** add clutter. Turn off *Auto date/time* in Options and add a single proper `dim_date` table marked as a date table.
2. **File paths are hard-coded** (`C:\Users\Admin\Downloads\...`), so refresh will fail on any other machine or in the Service. Use a parameter, or move the files to SharePoint, OneDrive or a database.
3. **`dim_city` is not directly related to `orders_clean`.** Filtering works through the customer chain, so an order is attributed to the customer's *current* city. If a customer can order in a different city, results will drift. Consider relating `orders_clean[city]` to `dim_city` directly.
4. **Unused and duplicate columns.** Hide technical columns (`city_raw`, `age_invalid`, `is_*` flags, `delivery_minutes` raw) from report view to keep the field list clean.
5. **Only 5 measures.** Add AOV, Cancellation Rate, Refund Rate, Discount % of GMV and MoM growth. They are cheap to build and all are supported by the existing columns.

### 6.3 Data quality

| Finding | Detail |
|---|---|
| Negative basket values | 112 orders with `basket_value ≤ 0` (excluded from GMV by `is_valid_basket`) |
| Delivery outliers | Raw max 811 min; cleaned version caps at 71.7 min. 5,248 orders have no valid delivery time (mostly cancelled/refunded) |
| Invalid ages | 68 customers (1.7%) flagged `age_invalid` |
| City spelling | 11 customers have `city_raw = "Bangalore"`, standardised to "Bengaluru" in `city` |
| Negative discount | Minimum `discount_amt` is −6.47 |
| Cancelled / refunded orders | 7,347 orders (6.9%), worth about ₹4.96 M in basket value, are outside GMV |

### 6.4 Metric interpretation cautions

- **Repeat Customer Rate of 98.2% is unusually high.** It counts anyone with 2 or more delivered orders over the full year, so over a 12-month window almost every active customer qualifies. It is not very discriminating. Consider a time-windowed version (for example, repeat within 30 days of first order) or an average orders-per-customer measure.
- **Churn Rate is a customer-level flag, not order-level.** It ignores the date slicer, since `customers_clean` has no relationship to a date table. It is also strongly tied to `account_status` (closed = 100%, dormant = 55%), so segments by status mostly restate the label rather than explain it.
- **GMV excludes cancelled and refunded orders** (net-style GMV). If stakeholders expect gross GMV, the figure would be about ₹71.9 M including those orders.
- **Matrix averages by city and hour** can look noisy in small cells (for example Jaipur at off-peak hours). Treat single-cell differences with caution.

